For the OOB, we need `end - beg == 3` and `data + beg` to be the last 3 bytes of the document. But `end` stops at the newline, so `end - beg` includes the newline if the line ends with `\n`.

Wait, let me reconsider. `end = beg; while (end < size && data[end - 1] != '\n') end++;`. This stops when `data[end-1] == '\n'`, i.e., `end` points just past the newline. So `end - beg` includes the newline.

So if the list item line is `- ` + 3 spaces + `\n`, then beg=2, end=6, end-beg=4. Sub-buffer = `   \n`. size=4. i=3, data[3] = `\n`. In bounds.

Hmm. So the trailing newline always makes it in bounds.

Let me reconsider. Maybe the OOB happens in a caller where the sub-buffer does NOT include the trailing newline. Let me look at the `parse_fencedcode` line 2297 again. The sub-buffer is `data + line_start` with size `i - line_start` where `i` is the position of `\n`. So the sub-buffer is the line WITHOUT the newline.

If the fenced code is NOT closed and the last line is exactly 3 spaces at the very end of the document, then `i` would reach `size` (no newline found). So the sub-buffer would be the last 3 bytes of the document WITHOUT the trailing newline.

But the document ends with `\n`. So the last line is `   \n`. In the loop, `i` scans to the `\n` and stops. So `i` = position of `\n`, sub-buffer = `   ` (3 bytes), and `data[3]` = `\n`. In bounds.

